A||||14||AutoXer||Beretta transmission vs Fiero transmission||||5||
Z||000000||AutoXer||08-20-2001||11:46 AM||||What are the differences between the Beretta GT/GTZ 5-speed and the Fiero Muncie 5-speed.<P>Why would you want to use one vs the other and what is involved in a conversion?<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR> [IMG] <A HREF="http://members.home.com/eeege//RedFieroRacing.jpg[/IMG]" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.com/eeege//RedFieroRacing.jpg[/IMG]</A>  <BR>"He ran out of talent about half way through the corner"||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||08-20-2001||12:23 PM||||As far as what's involved in using a non-Fiero Getrag in a Fiero, think Rodney Dickman is the expert.  Do a search for his website. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||lou_dias||08-20-2001||12:24 PM||||depends  on the year but some beretta/cavalier transmission also came with a 3.42 differential instead of the 3.65<P>it'd be a nice compromise between the fuel ecomony of the isuzu 5 speed (3.32) and our normal getrag ratio...||||reg||1
Z||000003||pherder||08-20-2001||12:54 PM||||     <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>depends  on the year but some beretta/cavalier transmission also came with a 3.42 differential instead of the 3.65<P>it'd be a nice compromise between the fuel ecomony of the isuzu 5 speed (3.32) and our normal getrag ratio...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Okay, lou_dias,<P>Where did you come up with that info???<P>The only two FDRs that GM had built for the Muncie/Getrag are 3.65 and 3.94 IAW all the GM race teams I have "talked" to while trying to find <B>ANY</B> other FDRs for that transaxle.<P>As for the differences ... <P>The Q4 Getrag/Muncies had a closer gear set than the V6 Getrag/Muncies.<P>The shift shaft (say that 3 time quickly     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) is 11 mm shorter on the FWD Getrag/Muncies (that is why Rodney developed his shift mechanism).<P>There are 3 different slave cylinder mechanisms.<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE>External slave that pushes an external arm (like our Fieros)<BR>External slave that pushes an <B>internal</B> arm (Rodney makes an adapter for them).<BR>Internal hydraulic throwout bearing ... is both things, a slave cylinder and throwout bearing ... requires a much larger clutch master cylinder.<BR></UL><P>Paul<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 08-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rodney||08-20-2001||03:38 PM||||There is the 284 used in the 3.4 dual cam. <P>ratios for 284 are<BR>1  3.46<BR>2  2.28<BR>3  1.48<BR>4  1.09<BR>5  0.72<BR>final:  3.67<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||08-20-2001||03:44 PM||||Don't forget the Z-24 Getrag. They use a gear driven VSS (actually a cable) vs. the optical VSS of the Beretta. You need to disassemble the trans to change types. So for a stock Fiero replacement, the Cavalier trans is easier to use, but for an engine conversion with an ECM that requires an optical VSS, the Beretta trans is easier to use. The Berettas and Cavaliers use an intermediate axle shaft, there are two different types of those. <P>As for why you would want to use a trans from a Beretta/Cavalier, they are far easier to find and therefore much cheaper. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||AutoXer||08-20-2001||05:32 PM||||Thanks guys<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://members.home.com/eeege/RedFieroRacing.jpg">    <BR>"He ran out of talent about half way through the corner"<BR><p>[This message has been edited by AutoXer (edited 08-20-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000007||Rogue_Ant||08-20-2001||09:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>As for why you would want to use a trans from a Beretta/Cavalier, they are far easier to find and therefore much cheaper. [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Um, what year Cavalier would be you reccomend to use?  I'm thinking of converting my 85GT Auto.<P>Rogue||||reg||5
Z||000008||rockcrawl||08-20-2001||09:30 PM||||According to Rodney's site they were only used in the Cavalier '87-'89 all with V6. I pulled three from the junkyards. I never bothered to check the year, I just know how to identify the Getrag by looking at it. Every Cavalier getrag I've seen has been identical and has the gear for the VSS and the external push-type slave. I also have a few from Berettas and one from a Q4 Grand-Am, and they all have optical VSS and different flavors of clutch release.||||reg||7
Z||000009||Rodney||08-20-2001||09:34 PM||||All FWD Getrags are the same except for a few items. As far as I know they all have the extension shaft on the passenger side. This makes the axles the same lenght and I would guess that helps in torque steer?? This extension axle and housing is easily removed. Most have the impedance cog wheel for the speedo. Some do have the gear drive but that is not important since changing it to a gear type is fairly easy. The other difference is the slave mounting. External like the Fiero or the internal arm. I would say the external arm is prefered but I have an adapter for the internal arm type. The internal arm style is used in Grand Prix, Grand Am and others but I do not have a list. When I call around to the salvage yards I ask for a 5 speed from a FWD V-6 GM. That covers most all variations and you can narrow it down from there. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||08-20-2001||09:43 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads\DCP01363.jpg"> <P>That's my collection of trans'. I like to have choices when I start a new project. There are 5 Getrags in that pic and I don't think any two of them are the same. Hey, I even have a slushbox in there just in case!||||reg||1
Z||000011||lou_dias||08-21-2001||09:39 AM||||pherder, I saw in a review of the 88 Cavalier Z24 that it cam with a 5 speed getrag and that it had the performance axle gear (3.65) vs. the standard on (3.42) over the base model Cavalier's.<P>Also, When I first got into fieros in 95 and picked up a kit car magazine, I read about a guy who put in a 3.23 gear.<P>the impression that I get is that final drive gear sets are fairly interchangeable.<P>3.23 and 3.42 gearsets for other GM transmissions are listed in a '97 Summit catalog that I had so I don't doubt that this can be done.<P>Also, in the Big Fiero Parts book under Fiero secrets where it mentions getting transmissions from beretta's and cavaliers, it warns about getting one with a 3.42 axle gear.<P>hell, won't <A HREF="http://www.isuzuperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.isuzuperformance.com</A>  sell you any ratio you want?<P>a 3.23 in a getrag with a V8 ought to make those v8 guys happy.<P>I did the math once and figured out that having a 3.42 gear ratio was the same as putting 225/60/15 tires on the rear wheels over the stock 215/60/15...||||reg||1
Z||000012||JayUSA77||08-21-2001||02:24 PM||||the only problem with paying all that money for the new ratio is if you have to much power and blow your transaxle up youre out of some bank<BR>j<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||Rodney||08-21-2001||03:43 PM||||I have had some email conversations with Getrag gmbh in Germany and they told me the only ratios were the 3.61 and the 3.94. I had asked them if any taller gears were available even if they were only used in Europe. I looked for those emails and I maybe did not save them. I'm thinking the other ratios are other (Isuzu?) transmissions?? Unless something else is available aftermarket? But usually the aftermarket goes lower not taller.||||reg||1
Z||000014||lou_dias||08-22-2001||09:17 AM||||I'll try to find the review of the Cavalier...  I think it was in the Dec 1987 issues for something like Car Enthusiast I think is the name of the mag, they also review the 88 Fiero GT.  It got great reviews.  I'll try to scan it and upload or email it to someone.  I think if you look in a book that transmission shops get, you'll find a 3.42 getrag and 3.65 listed under cavaliers and beretta's as well.||||reg||1
